 1.1 By [A2] and [F2], $P$ and $P^{-1}$ preserve the Lebesgue sigma-algebra and measure. Pullback by $P$ bijects nonnegative simple minorants and preserves their level-set measures, so the nonnegative integral is preserved by its defining supremum; applying positive and negative parts gives the same conclusion for the integrable tested scalar products in [F1]. Thus the time-first coordinates and the spatial-first weak definition give the same test integrals. For a test $\phi$ supported in $\mathcal O$, apply the product rule to the space-time current $(U^\pm,F_1^\pm,F_2^\pm,F_3^\pm)$ separately on $\mathcal O^-$ and $\mathcal O^+$. Coordinatewise integration by parts in $t,x_1,x_2,x_3$ gives $\int_{\mathcal O}(U\cdot\partial_t\phi+F:\nabla_x\phi+S\cdot\phi)=-\int_{\mathcal O^-}W^-\cdot\phi-\int_{\mathcal O^+}W^+\cdot\phi-\int_{J\times D}K_{\rm graph}\cdot\phi(t,x',h(t,x'))\,dx'\,dt$, where $W^\pm=\partial_tU^\pm+\operatorname{div}_xF^\pm-S^\pm$ and $K_{\rm graph}=[F_3]-\partial_th[U]-\nabla_{x'}h\cdot[F_{1,2}]$. Compact support removes all outer-boundary terms; the remaining graph term is the difference of the two side conormal fluxes. [given, F1, F2, A1, A2, construct, algebra]
 
